/// ProviderWorker — a supervised provider configuration and JWKS that
/// stay fresh.
///
/// `OAuthServer fromDiscovery:` loads discovery and the JWKS once, which
/// suits scripts, CLIs and the REPL. A long-running service can run a
/// `ProviderWorker` instead: it wraps `oidcc`'s
/// `oidcc_provider_configuration_worker`, which reloads the configuration
/// and JWKS when they expire (from the responses' cache headers), so
/// logins skip discovery. `server` answers an `OAuthServer` built from the
/// worker's current configuration and keys; use it wherever a
/// `fromDiscovery:` server goes.
///
/// When a token names a key the server does not have, validation asks the
/// worker to reload its JWKS, so the new key is kept for every later
/// login. Those reloads are limited to one per `#minJwksReloadInterval`
/// (default 30 s): within it, a token naming a key the worker lacks fails
/// validation without a fetch. If the worker's reload fails, validation
/// loads the keys itself.
///
/// The worker is an optimization, not a requirement: `fromDiscovery:`
/// works everywhere (ADR 0001, Open Question 9). `oidcc` starts no
/// processes of its own; the app owns this one.
///
/// Every load goes through the `http` package's `HTTPClient`, never
/// `httpc`, and each request times out after 10 s by default. The issuer
/// and every endpoint must be HTTPS. A failed load is retried with backoff
/// (1 s to 30 s by default), and `server` answers `provider_unavailable`
/// until a load succeeds.
///
/// `server` never waits on a reload, and makes no call to the `oidcc`
/// worker: it answers from the configuration and keys it last read. It
/// re-reads them in the background at most once per `#cacheTtl` (default
/// 60 s), and after an unknown-key reload. Reloads asked for by `refresh`
/// are rate-limited to one per `#backoffMin`.
///
/// While the provider is down, `refresh` does not add reloads: once a
/// load of the configuration or the JWKS has failed, `oidcc` keeps
/// retrying it with backoff, and `refresh` leaves that part alone until a
/// load of it succeeds. Then `refresh` reloads it again. It also leaves
/// alone a part whose load is still running. Unknown-key reloads skip the
/// worker the same way while its JWKS load is running or failed, and load
/// the keys directly instead.
///
/// The unknown-key reload draws from the operation's deadline (`#deadline`,
/// default 4500 ms, under the 5 s actor-call timeout; see "Deadlines" in
/// `OAuthServer`), counted from the start of the call that validates, so a
/// slow token request uses part of it. The wait for the worker and any
/// direct load share it; once it is spent no load starts, and the call
/// answers a `transport` error that says the deadline was exceeded. The
/// worker's own background loads are not bounded by it.
///
/// The worker holds no secrets; `sys:get_status` shows only its issuer and
/// the `oidcc` worker's pid.
///
/// Options, as for `OAuthServer fromDiscovery:preset:options:`
/// (`#maxBodyBytes`, `#timeout`, `#deadline` (an Integer > 0, the
/// deadline of each operation on a server it answers), test-only
/// `#allowUnsafeHttp`, which also needs the test flag described there),
/// plus
/// `#backoffMin` and `#backoffMax` (ms) and `#backoffType`
/// (`#exponential`, `#random`, `#random_exponential`) for failed loads,
/// and `#cacheTtl` and `#minJwksReloadInterval` (ms, Integers >= 0).
///
/// ## In a supervision tree
/// `supervisionSpecFor:` takes the same Dictionary as `startWith:`
/// (`#issuer`, `#preset`, `#options`). The supervisor restarts the worker
/// if it, or the `oidcc` worker it runs, crashes.
/// ```beamtalk
/// Supervisor subclass: AppSup
///   childSpecs => #(
///     ((ProviderWorker supervisionSpecFor: #{#preset => ProviderPresets gitlab})
///       withId: #gitlab
///       withRestart: #permanent),
///     WebServer)
///
/// // later, in a request handler:
/// worker := (AppSup current childWithId: #gitlab) unwrap
/// server := worker server unwrap
/// ```
///
/// ## Examples
/// ```beamtalk
/// worker := (ProviderWorker preset: ProviderPresets gitlab) unwrap
/// server := worker server unwrap
/// server issuer                 // => "https://gitlab.com"
/// worker stop
/// ```
Actor subclass: ProviderWorker native: beamtalk_oauth_provider_worker

  /// Start a worker for `issuer`, with no preset.
  class issuer: issuer :: String -> Result(ProviderWorker, OAuthError) =>
    self
      issuer: issuer
      preset: #{}
      options: #{}

  /// Start a worker for the provider a preset names (`#issuer`), with its
  /// quirks.
  class preset: preset :: Dictionary -> Result(ProviderWorker, OAuthError) =>
    self preset: preset options: #{}

  /// As `preset:`, with options.
  class preset: preset :: Dictionary options: opts :: Dictionary -> Result(ProviderWorker, OAuthError) =>
    self startWith: #{#preset => preset, #options => opts}

  /// Start a worker for `issuer`, applying the preset's
  /// `#documentOverrides` and endpoints, with options (see the class
  /// comment).
  class issuer: issuer :: String preset: preset :: Dictionary options: opts :: Dictionary -> Result(ProviderWorker, OAuthError) =>
    self startWith: #{#issuer => issuer, #preset => preset, #options => opts}

  /// Start a worker from a Dictionary: `#issuer` (or the preset's),
  /// `#preset` (default `#{}`) and `#options` (default `#{}`). A bad
  /// issuer or option answers `Result error:` and starts nothing.
  ///
  /// The worker is linked to the caller, as any `spawnWith:` is; in a
  /// service, start it under a supervisor with `supervisionSpecFor:`.
  class startWith: config :: Dictionary -> Result(ProviderWorker, OAuthError) =>
    (self spawnArgsFor: config) map: [:full | self spawnWith: full]

  /// A `SupervisionSpec` that starts a worker from `config` (as
  /// `startWith:` takes it). Refine it with `withId:`, `withRestart:`, ...
  /// The config is checked here, so a bad one raises when the supervisor
  /// builds its child specs, before anything starts.
  class supervisionSpecFor: config :: Dictionary -> SupervisionSpec =>
    (self spawnArgsFor: config) ifOk: [:full |
      self supervisionSpec withArgs: full
    ] ifError: [:err |
      Error signal: "ProviderWorker: {err code}: {err description}"
    ]

  /// The backing module's start config for `config` (as `startWith:`
  /// takes it), or the `OAuthError` for a bad one.
  class spawnArgsFor: config :: Dictionary -> Result(Dictionary, OAuthError) =>
    preset := config at: #preset ifAbsent: [#{}]
    (preset isKindOf: Dictionary) ifFalse: [
      ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "#preset must be a Dictionary")
    ]
    issuer := config at: #issuer ifAbsent: [preset at: #issuer ifAbsent: [nil]]
    (issuer isKindOf: String) ifFalse: [
      ^Result error: (OAuthError config: "no #issuer, and the preset names none")
    ]
    full := #{
      #issuer => issuer,
      #client => HTTPClient,
      #preset => preset,
      #options => (config at: #options ifAbsent: [#{}])
    }
    problem := Erlang beamtalk_oauth_provider_worker check: full
    problem isNil ifFalse: [^Result error: (self errorFor: problem)]
    Result ok: full

  /// The `OAuthError` for a config problem from the backing module's
  /// `check/1`.
  class errorFor: problem :: Tuple -> OAuthError =>
    (problem at: 1) =:= #config ifTrue: [
      OAuthError config: (problem at: 2)
    ] ifFalse: [OAuthError fromOidcc: problem]

  /// An `OAuthServer` from the worker's current configuration and JWKS.
  /// Answers at once from the last configuration and keys read (see the
  /// class comment on `#cacheTtl`); only
  /// before the first one does it wait (up to 4.5 s) for a load. Answers
  /// `provider_unavailable` while no load has succeeded, and
  /// `insecure_endpoint` if the configuration names a non-HTTPS endpoint.
  server -> Result(OAuthServer, OAuthError) => self delegate

  /// Reload the configuration and JWKS now, waiting up to 4.5 s for both.
  /// Normally the worker reloads on its own when they expire. Within
  /// `#backoffMin` of the last reload this starts none. A failed reload
  /// is not reported here: the worker keeps its current configuration and
  /// keys, `oidcc` retries the load with backoff, and the failure is
  /// logged.
  refresh -> Nil => self delegate

  /// The issuer the worker was started with.
  issuer -> String => self delegate

  /// The pid of the `oidcc_provider_configuration_worker` it runs.
  oidccWorker -> Pid => self delegate
